    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study deals with the interpretation of in-situ combustion thermal oil recovery injection well falloff tests using the pseudo-steady state concept. This concept is based on the fact that during the combustion process the inner (swept) region around the gas injection well is usually filled with high mobility gas while the unswept region contains low mobility fluid. Due to the large contrast between the conductivity of the gas in the swept volume and that in the unswept sand ahead, transient effects caused by the !swept volume would be characteristic of a section of very high transmissivity. Hence, the swept zone will behave like a separate \"closed \" reservoir, and attain a temporary pseudo-steady state during falloff testing. A Cartesian graph of pressure versus shut-in time during this period will yield a straight line whose slope is related to the swept volume.The use of the pseudo-steady state concept for the determination of the swept volume has been investigated with two-zone analytic reservoir models, and results show that the correct swept volume may be obtained using this concept. A three-zone model is developed in this study for use in further investigation. With the three-zone model, the effect of a transition zone between the swept zone and unswept zone may be included. Results from this model show that the transition zone affects the Cartesian straight line slope, although the effect is small. Also, the effect of the mobility ratio and diffusivity ratio between the inner and outer zones is studied.The analytical models neglected temperature and saturation distribution effects. These effects are included in this study using finite difference simulators which model more realistic temperature and saturation distributions in a combustion process. The swept volume calculated from the analysis of simulated falloff tests agrees with the simulated swept volume. However, this swept volume includes a high gas saturation zone ahead of the burning front as well as the burned volume. Also, correct interpretation of the simulated falloff test requires the use of an effective compressibility and temperature in the swept volume. The burned volume, which is the parameter of interest required for calculation of fuel concentration and volumetric air requirement may be estimated from the swept volume using an analytical heat transfer model. The use of an analytical heat model makes it possible to avoid some uncertainties in current finite difference formulations, yet consider several factors that affect the growth of the swept volume. Field tests are analyzed in this study and consideration of design of such tests is discussed.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
